Exactly — **O(1)**.

Your reasoning is right: removing the last element doesn’t force the rest of the list to shift, so the amount of work stays constant even if the list gets much bigger.

One tiny wording tweak: index `3` doesn’t really need to be “fixed”; that element is simply removed, and the remaining indexes stay where they are.

You’ve got the core list-operation idea now. Let’s test one that mixes access and search:

```python
nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]

x = nums[2]
print(50 in nums)
```

What is the overall time complexity of these two operations together?